     "type": "application",
     "title": "Audit Your Own Neighborhood",
     "content": "You can apply this framework without waiting for a city government to adopt a formal Blue Zones Project license, by treating your own neighborhood as a small policy audit. Start with a walkability check: time how long it takes to walk, not drive, to the three destinations you visit most often, such as a grocery store, workplace, or a friend's home. If the honest answer is that none of them are walkable, that is a built-environment finding, not a personal failing, and it tells you where to focus advocacy or relocation decisions. Next, audit your food environment within that same radius: count how many outlets sell fresh produce versus how many sell only packaged and fast food, and note which one is more convenient to your daily route by default. Then audit social infrastructure: identify whether there is a free or low-cost third place, a library, park, community center, or walkable coffee shop, within your life radius where an unplanned social interaction or a recurring group meeting could plausibly happen without a car. If any of these three audits comes up short, the built-environment argument suggests the highest-leverage response is not a personal resolution but a structural one: attending a city planning or zoning board meeting, supporting a local Complete Streets or Safe Routes to School initiative, or advocating for a produce stocking requirement at a nearby corner store. None of these require you personally to have more discipline. They require changing what the default option is for everyone in your radius, which is the entire point of the built-environment argument: fix the environment once, and the healthy behavior takes care of itself for as long as the environment stays fixed.",
